        <type>std::vector&lt; <ref refid="struct_tryll_1_1_client_1_1_tryll_client_1_1_engine_selection" kindref="compound">EngineSelection</ref> &gt;</type>
        <definition>std::vector&lt;EngineSelection&gt; Tryll::Client::TryllClient::SessionConfig::engines</definition>
        <argsstring></argsstring>
        <name>engines</name>
        <qualifiedname>Tryll::Client::TryllClient::SessionConfig::engines</qualifiedname>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Optional, advisory hint: the engines this session expects to touch, as (role, engine) pairs. Leaving it empty is legal and unremarkable — a model&apos;s engine comes from the server&apos;s catalog, so a load works whether or not this named the engine it needs.</para>
<para>What it buys is timing: the server initialises the named engines&apos; backends at CreateSession instead of at first use, which is worth doing when that cost is large (an ORT CUDA cold start was measured in tens of seconds). A harness that wants determinism about which engines a run touched can also state it here; the editors derive it from their model manifest and expose no engine control.</para>
<para>Only an out-of-range ordinal is rejected (<computeroutput>InvalidEngineSet</computeroutput>, 2005). A duplicate pair is logged and ignored. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
